  "query_preview": "..Considerable clouds were across southern Arizona\nthis morning with radar showing very weak echos which are most\nlikely virga since its rather dry at the surface with dewpoints \nin the 20s and 30s. PWATs across the area were mostly between \n0.75\" east to 0.90\" west. Ensembles are indicating that the far \neastern areas will see an increase in PWATs today which may lead\nto a very slight chance of a mainly dry thunderstorm along the \nAZ/NM border.\n\nOtherwise the main forecast items for the upcoming work week\nwill be the heat and the gradual daily westward expansion of \nthunderstorm chances as an active monsoon pattern sets up.\n\nHeat: \nThe upper high centered over NM last night will gradually\nexpand and strengthen over the area early this week leading to \nhotter high temperatures and major HeatRisk, especially on \nTuesday and Wednesday, where an Extreme Heat Watch has been \nissued for the lower deserts.\n\nThunderstorms:\nMonday: 20-30% chance of afternoon/evening showers and\nthunderstorm mainly east of a Safford to Bisbee line. With the\nlower levels still on the dry side, main concern will be strong\ngusty winds, possibly as high as 50 mph, and blowing dust.\n\nTuesday: With the upper high over central AZ, the steering flow\nwill be out of E-NE, allowing any higher terrain thunderstorms\neast of Tucson to propagate into the valleys. Again main concern\nwill be strong gusty winds and blowing dust.\n\nWednesday: Similar to Tuesday except with now with the added \nmoisture across the areas, localized heavy rainers and increased\nchances for flash flooding are now possible.\n\nThursday through Saturday: Daily chances of showers and\nthunderstorms with the usual threats that occur during a monsoon\nday.",
